1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,974
iTantra is an offline, multilingual voice walkie-
talkie for India — speech becomes text, text

2
00:00:05,974 --> 00:00:08,961
becomes speech, when the network is gone.

3
00:00:08,961 --> 00:00:14,922
Voice is heavy: a raw spoken sentence needs about
two hundred fifty six kilobits per second, but

4
00:00:14,922 --> 00:00:20,883
disaster links carry only a few hundred bits per
second. And a text-only alert misses the many

5
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:22,636
people who cannot read it.

6
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:29,362
So iTantra sends only the text of what was said — a
couple hundred bytes — over Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, or

7
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:34,742
a LoRa bridge, and speaks it back on the other
phone, in the listener's own language.

8
00:00:34,742 --> 00:00:40,786
Under the hood: on-device speech recognition, a
self-healing link that falls back from Wi-Fi to

9
00:00:40,786 --> 00:00:47,233
Bluetooth to a long-range LoRa bridge, and a full
deployment reaching phones, relay nodes, and an

10
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:48,442
offline control room.

11
00:00:48,442 --> 00:00:55,492
Every model is open source and fully offline:
SraVaani speech recognition from ARTPARK and I I Sc,

12
00:00:55,492 --> 00:01:01,713
and Indic-Mio and VITS Rasa text-to-speech from
SPRING Lab I I T Madras and AI4Bharat.

13
00:01:01,713 --> 00:01:08,669
And it's built for real phones: under two percent
idle CPU, well under a gigabyte of memory in the

14
00:01:08,669 --> 00:01:13,429
LITE profile, and sentences as small as sixty two
bytes on the wire.

15
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:15,130
Here it is on a real phone.

16
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:31,130
ALERT priority — max volume, cannot be interrupted

17
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:49,130
One-tap SOS — location sent and spoken

18
00:01:49,130 --> 00:01:55,781
iTantra — built for the moment the towers go silent.
Smart India Hackathon twenty twenty six, ISRO

19
00:01:55,781 --> 00:01:58,520
problem statement two six one seven three.

